--- /dev/null
+++ a/docs/UPDATES.md
@@ -0,0 +1,75 @@
+# Updates (Sparkle)
+
+SwiftMail updates itself with [Sparkle 2](https://sparkle-project.org). The app
+checks `appcast.xml` in this repository, downloads the release archive from
+GitHub Releases, and verifies an EdDSA signature before installing anything.
+
+The app is sandboxed, so Sparkle installs through its `Installer.xpc` service.
+That is what `SUEnableInstallerLauncherService` in `swift-mail/Info.plist` and
+the `mach-lookup.global-name` exception in `swift_mail.entitlements` are for —
+remove either and updates download but never install.
+
+## One-time setup
+
+Updates stay disabled until the app carries a public key. `SoftwareUpdate`
+checks for one at launch and simply doesn't start the updater without it, so a
+key-less build has a greyed-out "Check for Updates…" rather than an error.
+
+1. Fetch Sparkle's tools (they are not part of the app build):
+
+```bash
+curl -fsSL -o /tmp/sparkle.tar.xz https://github.com/sparkle-project/Sparkle/releases/download/2.9.6/Sparkle-2.9.6.tar.xz && mkdir -p /tmp/sparkle && tar -xf /tmp/sparkle.tar.xz -C /tmp/sparkle
+```
+
+2. Generate the key pair. The private key goes into your login Keychain; the
+   public key is printed:
+
+```bash
+/tmp/sparkle/bin/generate_keys
+```
+
+3. Paste the printed public key into `SUPublicEDKey` in
+   `swift-mail/Info.plist`, and commit it. It is meant to be public.
+
+4. Export the private key and add it to the repository as the
+   `SPARKLE_PRIVATE_KEY` secret. Anyone holding it can sign an update this app
+   will trust, so it never belongs in the repository:
+
+```bash
+/tmp/sparkle/bin/generate_keys -x -   # prints the private key to stdout
+```
+
+## Repository secrets
+
+| Secret | Required | What it does |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| `SPARKLE_PRIVATE_KEY` | for updates | Signs the release archive. Without it the workflow still builds and publishes, but writes no appcast entry, so nobody is offered the update. |
+| `MACOS_CERTIFICATE_P12` | for signed builds | Base64 of your Developer ID Application certificate (`base64 -i cert.p12`). |
+| `MACOS_CERTIFICATE_PASSWORD` | with the above | The `.p12` export password. |
+| `MACOS_TEAM_ID` | with the above | Ten-character Apple team identifier. |
+| `NOTARY_APPLE_ID` | for notarization | Apple ID used with `notarytool`. |
+| `NOTARY_PASSWORD` | with the above | App-specific password for that Apple ID. |
+
+Without the signing secrets the workflow falls back to the unsigned build it
+made before. An unsigned build is fine for trying the app out, but Sparkle
+cannot meaningfully update one: macOS refuses to launch an unsigned replacement
+that arrives from the internet, and the sandbox entitlements above are only
+applied to a signed bundle.
+
+## Cutting a release
+
+Push a tag:
+
+```bash
+git tag v1.1.0 && git push origin v1.1.0
+```
+
+The workflow then builds, signs and notarizes if the secrets are present,
+attaches `SwiftMail-macos.zip` to the release, signs the archive with the
+Sparkle key, and commits the new `appcast.xml` entry to `main`.
+
+Versions come from the tag and the run number: `MARKETING_VERSION` is the tag
+without its leading `v` (what people see), and `CURRENT_PROJECT_VERSION` is the
+workflow run number (what Sparkle compares). The run number only ever goes up,
+which is the property Sparkle needs and the one a hand-edited build number
+tends to lose.
